\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Aggregate robust accuracy on the CIFAR-10 evaluation suite}}{59}{table.caption.32}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Effect-size summary for AttackDRO++ (Ours) against each baseline across 20 paired random-seed runs. $\mitDelta $ in percentage points; negative values mean the baseline is stronger.}}{60}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Paired aggregate robustness comparison between AttackDRO++ (Ours) and each baseline across 20 paired random-seed runs. \(\mitDelta \) is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the corresponding baseline in percentage points. The \(p\)-value is from a two-sided paired \(t\)-test.}}{61}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Multiple-comparison-corrected paired significance tests for AttackDRO++ (Ours) against each baseline across $n=20$ paired seeds. $\mitDelta $ is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the corresponding baseline in percentage points. The table reports bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals, seed-level win counts, Cohen's paired effect size $d_z$, paired $t$-test $p$-values, Wilcoxon signed-rank $p$-values, and Holm-corrected Wilcoxon $p$-values. Holm correction is applied within each baseline comparison across the seven reported metrics. AutoAttack is excluded from this table and reported separately in Section~\ref {subsec:ch6_hardest_evaluation_cases}.}}{62}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Per-attack robust accuracy (\%) for all four methods across 20 paired random-seed runs. Bold cells indicate the highest accuracy for each attack. The right-hand $\mitDelta $ and $p$ columns report the paired comparison of AttackDRO++ (Ours) against Multi-AT. AttackDRO++ (Ours) wins the highest score on 4 of 8 attacks; the two single-attack baselines together account for the remaining 4, each winning on the family they were trained against. $\mitDelta $ values use the bold-marker convention $^*$ for $p<0.05$ and $^{**}$ for $p<0.01$.}}{64}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Worst(8) and 512-sample AutoAttack sanity results across 20 paired random-seed runs. Worst(8) is the minimum robust accuracy over the eight non-AutoAttack evaluation attacks. For AttackDRO++ $-$ Multi-AT, the paired differences are $-0.003$~pp on Worst(8) ($p=0.971$) and $-0.001$~pp on AutoAttack ($p=0.998$). Bold values indicate the highest method.}}{65}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Full-test AutoAttack-$\ell _\infty $ results on the selected $n=5$ paired seed panel. The paired difference is AttackDRO++ $-$ Multi-AT: $-0.006$~pp, with $p=0.9584$.}}{65}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Variance reduction across 20 paired random-seed runs. \(\mitsigma \) is the standard deviation of the per-seed metric. AttackDRO++ substantially reduces seed-to-seed variation on Mean(8), held-out averages, and held-out norm-specific metrics, while Worst(8) and AutoAttack standard deviations remain similar across methods.}}{66}{table.caption.43}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Per-(class $\times $ attack) whitebox comparison of AttackDRO++ against each baseline. $\mitDelta $ is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the baseline. Positive cells count the number of attack--class cells where $\mitDelta >0$ out of 80 total cells.}}{71}{table.caption.53}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Whitebox sanity check from the graybox pipeline. The five-random-seed columns use diagonal graybox entries, where surrogate and target are the same checkpoint. The 20-random-seed columns report the main whitebox results from Table~\ref {tab:ch6_aggregate_results}. Bold values indicate the best method for each metric.}}{76}{table.caption.60}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.11}{\ignorespaces Paired graybox comparisons. Each row uses $n=200$ paired target--surrogate--attack triples. Positive $\mitDelta $ favors the reference method named in the panel heading.}}{80}{table.caption.64}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.12}{\ignorespaces Per-attack symmetric graybox comparison between AttackDRO++ and Multi-AT. Each attack uses \(n=25\) paired seed combinations. Significance is reported in the \(p\)-value column.}}{81}{table.caption.66}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.13}{\ignorespaces Cross-method graybox accuracy by attack family. Values are off-diagonal target accuracy (\%) averaged over the \(n=5\) panel. Bold values indicate the best method for each metric.}}{82}{table.caption.67}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.14}{\ignorespaces Whitebox--graybox gap by target method on the \(n=5\) panel. The gap is cross-method graybox Mean(8) minus diagonal whitebox Mean(8).}}{82}{table.caption.69}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.15}{\ignorespaces Per-(class \(\times \) attack) graybox tests comparing AttackDRO++ against each baseline. \(\mitDelta \) is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the baseline.}}{85}{table.caption.74}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.16}{\ignorespaces AttackDRO++ (Ours) gain decomposition. Values are percentage-point changes.}}{88}{table.caption.79}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.17}{\ignorespaces Number-of-clusters ablation for AttackDRO++ with anchor strength $\mitlambda _{\mathrm {DRO}}=0.35$. Values are robust accuracy (\%) reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviation across available runs. Mean(8) and Worst(8) are computed over the eight non-AutoAttack attacks; AutoAttack-$\ell _\infty $ is reported separately. Bold values indicate the best setting for each metric.}}{90}{table.caption.81}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.18}{\ignorespaces Anchor-strength ablation for AttackDRO++ with $K=4$. Values are robust accuracy (\%) reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviation across three runs. Mean(8) and Worst(8) are computed over the eight non-AutoAttack attacks; AutoAttack-$\ell _\infty $ is reported separately. Bold values indicate the best setting for each metric.}}{92}{table.caption.83}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.19}{\ignorespaces Cluster-refresh schedule ablation for AttackDRO++ with $\mitlambda _{\mathrm {DRO}}=0.35$, gradient fingerprints, and $K=4$. Values are robust accuracy (\%) reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviation across three runs. Mean(8) and Worst(8) are computed over the eight non-AutoAttack attacks; AutoAttack-$\ell _\infty $ is reported separately. Bold values indicate the best setting for each metric.}}{93}{table.caption.85}%
